<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virgil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">70–<a href="/years/19-bce">19 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Publius Vergilius Maro, Vergil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virgil was a Roman poet of the Augustan age, author of the Eclogues, the Georgics and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, the epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s legendary Trojan origins. The Aeneid became the national poem of Rome and a school text for centuries, and its lines were quoted on coins and inscriptions. Virgil lived much of his life near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where he was buried.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/virgil.jpg" alt="Virgil" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boyd Dwyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgil_mosaic_in_the_Bardo_National_Museum_(Tunis)_(12241228546).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom produced a distinctive literature in Scots, from John Barbour’s verse epic The Bruce of the 1370s to the makars of the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, Robert Henryson, William Dunbar and Gavin Douglas, whose translation of <b>Virgil</b>’s Aeneid was completed in 1513.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vergil</b>’s Aeneid gave the Imperial vision of the past, Pliny the Elder wrote a sceptical history of magic, and Apuleius’s The Golden Ass almost certainly describes the mysteries of Isis.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Virgil</b>’s Aeneid gave Rome a national epic, Horace wrote odes and satires, Ovid retold the myths of transformation in the Metamorphoses, and Livy wrote a history of Rome from its foundation.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Without access to Homer, Dante took <b>Virgil</b>, Lucan, Ovid and Statius as models, Virgil above all.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune runs through later music and writing: Hubert Parry's Three Chorale Fantasias, Felix Mendelssohn's Piano Trio in C minor, <b>Virgil</b> Thomson's score for The Plow that Broke the Plains and Paul Hindemith's Trauermusik (both 1936), and Benjamin Britten's cantata St Nicolas (1948).</blockquote><a href="/songs/old-100th">Old 100th</a></li><li><blockquote>The legend, told in Homer and <b>Virgil</b>, shaped European literature for millennia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-24">24 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Andes, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.82&amp;year=-37" aria-label="Andes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4807 571 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Virgil"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5257 841h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5257 841h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.82&amp;year=-37">What was here in 37 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Arms, and the man I sing, who, forc’d by fate, And haughty Juno’s unrelenting hate, Expell’d and exil’d, left the Trojan shore.&#8221;</p><footer>Aeneid, book 1 &#183; tr. John Dryden, 1697 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/228/pg228-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Happy, who had the skill to understand Nature's hid causes, and beneath his feet All terrors cast, and death's relentless doom, And the loud roar of greedy Acheron.&#8221;</p><footer>Georgics, book 2 &#183; tr. James Rhoades, 1881 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/232/pg232-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Fast flies meanwhile the irreparable hour, As point to point our charmed round we trace.&#8221;</p><footer>Georgics, book 3 &#183; tr. James Rhoades, 1881 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/232/pg232-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But, Rome, ’tis thine alone, with awful sway, To rule mankind, and make the world obey, Disposing peace and war by thy own majestic way; To tame the proud, the fetter’d slave to free: These are imperial arts, and worthy thee.&#8221;</p><footer>Aeneid, book 6 &#183; tr. John Dryden, 1697 &#183; spoken by Anchises to his son Aeneas in the underworld &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/228/pg228-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;An hour will come, with pleasure to relate Your sorrows past, as benefits of Fate.&#8221;</p><footer>Aeneid, book 1 &#183; tr. John Dryden, 1697 &#183; spoken by Aeneas to his shipwrecked men &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/228/pg228-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
